From ff0d31b7b7b4569226e6122fbdf317201d82dec1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ed <edimoldovan@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:55:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 22/23] phy: apple: add a T6050 USB 2.0 host PHY and use it on
 J714c

The T6050 (M5 Pro/Max) Type-C PHY is a new generation that Linux does not
drive yet, so USB 2.0 host depended on the loader putting each port into
host mode before the kernel started. The new apple,t6050-usb2-phy driver
does that itself in phy_init(), which xhci-plat calls before its host
controller reset: usb2phy out of reset and running, pipe handler on the
dummy backend, usb2phy host bits, gadget stopped, dwc3 core soft reset,
port capability host, PIPE parked, PHY auto-suspend off (the loader's
usb_t6050_host_prep sequence). It touches only the usb2phy block (atc-phy
reg 0), the pipe handler (usb-drd reg 3) and the dwc3 core, never the
SuperSpeed/CIO blocks. A port is flipped up to three times until PORTSC
shows a connection.

t6050-j714c-usb2.dtsi gets one PHY node per port and points each
generic-xhci node at it.

Checked on J714c with a loader built with T6050_NO_HOST_PREP (record all
zeros): all three ports come up in USB 2.0 host mode; a stick in port 1
connects at the first flip and enumerates (usb-storage, sda); port 2,
which carried the loader's proxy, binds without the old xHCI reset
timeout.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit ae65f0d3a88e5f839bdeba2bc52e381c53e034aa)
